{"rows":[{"n":1,"file":"topics/01-java-core-jvm.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":2,"file":"topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":3,"file":"topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":4,"file":"topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":5,"file":"topics/05-db-core-index-lock.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":6,"file":"topics/06-db-scaling.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":7,"file":"topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":8,"file":"topics/08-message-queue.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":9,"file":"topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":12,"file":"topics/12-architecture-patterns.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":13,"file":"topics/13-security-oauth2.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":14,"file":"topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":15,"file":"topics/15-network-i-o-models.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":16,"file":"topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.json","topic_type":"algorithm","system_design_items":["16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q1","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q3","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1"]},{"n":17,"file":"topics/17-rest-api-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":18,"file":"topics/18-query-optimization.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":19,"file":"topics/19-dsa-leetcode.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":20,"file":"topics/20-observability-sre.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":21,"file":"topics/21-linux-production-debug.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":22,"file":"topics/22-low-level-design-ood.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":23,"file":"topics/23-java-concurrency-coding.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":24,"file":"topics/24-domain-driven-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":25,"topic_type":"microservice","file":"topics/25-microservice.json"},{"n":26,"file":"topics/26-testing-strategy.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":28,"file":"topics/28-distributed-lock-lease.json","topic_type":"design"}],"row":{"n":4,"file":"topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux.json","topic_type":"design"},"meta":{"topic_type":"design","key":"04-rest-grpc-webflux","vi":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux","title":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & lập trình reactive (WebFlux)","intro":"Cách các service nói chuyện và xử lý I/O ở mức cao. Trọng tâm: chọn đúng protocol, hiểu reactive vs virtual threads, và thiết kế API sạch.","tags":["REST idempotency","gRPC/Protobuf","HTTP/2","Mono/Flux","Backpressure","Reactive vs VT","Versioning"]},"en":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux","title":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","intro":"How services talk to each other and handle I/O at a high level. The focus: picking the right protocol, reactive versus virtual threads, and designing a clean API.","tags":["REST idempotency","gRPC/Protobuf","HTTP/2","Mono/Flux","Backpressure","Reactive vs VT","Versioning"]},"created_at":"2026-07-31","updated_at":"2026-10-25"},"topicMeta":{"1":{"en":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"},"vi":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"}},"2":{"en":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"},"vi":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"}},"3":{"en":{"label":"Spring Boot deep dive + build"},"vi":{"label":"Spring Boot deep + build"}},"4":{"en":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"},"vi":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"}},"5":{"en":{"label":"DB core: indexes & locking"},"vi":{"label":"DB core: index & lock"}},"6":{"en":{"label":"DB scaling"},"vi":{"label":"DB scaling"}},"7":{"en":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"},"vi":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"}},"8":{"en":{"label":"Message queue"},"vi":{"label":"Message queue"}},"9":{"en":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"}},"10":{"en":{"label":"System design + rate limit"},"vi":{"label":"System design + rate limit"}},"11":{"en":{"label":"System design cases"},"vi":{"label":"System design cases"}},"12":{"en":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"},"vi":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"}},"13":{"en":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"},"vi":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"}},"14":{"en":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"},"vi":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"}},"15":{"en":{"label":"Network & I/O models"},"vi":{"label":"Network & I/O models"}},"16":{"en":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"},"vi":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"}},"17":{"en":{"label":"REST API design"},"vi":{"label":"Thiết kế REST API"}},"18":{"en":{"label":"Query optimisation"},"vi":{"label":"Tối ưu query"}},"19":{"en":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"},"vi":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"}},"20":{"en":{"label":"Observability & SRE"},"vi":{"label":"Observability & SRE"}},"21":{"en":{"label":"Linux & production debug"},"vi":{"label":"Linux & production debug"}},"22":{"en":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"},"vi":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"}},"23":{"en":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"},"vi":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"}},"24":{"en":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"},"vi":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"}},"25":{"en":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"},"vi":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"}},"26":{"en":{"label":"Testing"},"vi":{"label":"Testing"}},"27":{"en":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"},"vi":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"}},"28":{"en":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"}}},"reviewedAt":{"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q1":"2026-09-12","04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q2":"2026-09-12","04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q2":"2026-09-12","04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q3":"2026-09-12","04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q4":"2026-09-12","04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q5":"2026-09-12","04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q6":"2026-09-12","04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q4":"2026-09-12","04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q5":"2026-09-12","04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q1":"2026-09-12"},"lang":"en","body":{"sections":[{"title":"Protocols","items":[{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q1","q":"REST principles, HTTP method idempotency, and correct status codes?","a":"REST: resource + representation + operations via standard methods, stateless.\n\n[[g:Idempotency]]:\n- GET/PUT/DELETE/HEAD should be idempotent (calling repeatedly gives the same result)\n- POST [[r:is not]] (creates something new each time) → payment POST needs an `Idempotency-Key`\n\n**Status codes:**\n- 200/201/204 success\n- 400 bad input · 401/403 auth · 404\n- 409 conflict · 422 is a common validation convention (document your 4xx policy) · 429 rate limit\n- 5xx server error\n\nUsing the right status code helps the client retry sensibly.\n\n:::deep\n- PUT is idempotent (sets absolute state) but [[r:be careful with 'increment/decrement'-style PUT]] (not idempotent)\n- distinguish *safe* (GET/HEAD don't change state) vs *idempotent* (calling multiple times = calling once)\n- status codes are a [[g:contract]] with the client/monitoring: 409 (seat already held), a documented 422 validation policy, 429 + `Retry-After`, and 503 (degraded)\n- [[r:returning 200 for everything]] with the error embedded in the body breaks retry/alerting/circuit-breaker logic\n- ETag + `If-Match` for optimistic concurrency over HTTP\n\nDeep dive: business error codes in the body plus the correct status help the client automate retries — important for an OTA's B2B partner API.\n\n- The hands-on build: going from a use case to resources and naming the URI (17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q1), then mapping non-CRUD actions like cancel/refund onto REST (17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q2).\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q2","q":"REST vs gRPC — what's the difference and when to use which?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>REST/JSON</th><th>gRPC</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Format</td><td>text (JSON)</td><td>binary (Protobuf)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transport</td><td>HTTP/1.1-2</td><td>HTTP/2 multiplexing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Contract</td><td>OpenAPI (optional)</td><td>.proto generates code</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Streaming</td><td>limited (SSE)</td><td>4 kinds, bidirectional</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good for</td><td>public API, browser/mobile</td><td>internal low-latency microservices</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nIn practice, a gateway translating [[g:REST/JSON at an edge]] to gRPC for selected internal calls is a common topology, not a law.\n\nBrowser support, public tooling, proxies, streaming, observability, organizational boundaries, or contract governance may lead to REST end to end, gRPC with grpc-web/transcoding at the edge, or another protocol.\n\nChoose per boundary and measured contract; gRPC is hard to use directly from a browser without grpc-web or a proxy.\n\n:::tip Bottom line\nREST often fits the public edge and gRPC often fits selected internal low-latency or streaming calls, but the boundary is workload- and organization-dependent. Protobuf supports compatible evolution through field numbers when its field-evolution rules are followed.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- Protobuf's wire representation can reduce bytes or CPU for some schemas, but the result depends on payload shape, compression, runtime, and client/library; benchmark the deployed path before claiming a win.\n- safe evolution still depends on field-number rules: add compatible fields, and never reuse or reinterpret removed numbers.\n- [[r:Gotcha]]: gRPC is hard from a browser (grpc-web/Envoy); L7 load balancing needs a proxy that understands HTTP/2 (sticky streams)\n- propagate the deadline through every hop and retry only under an explicit idempotency policy and bounded budget (15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q2), (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4)\n- OTA deep dive: REST gateway toward partners/B2C, gRPC internally between search/pricing/inventory to cut latency & load\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q3","q":"What are gRPC's streaming modes?","a":"Four kinds:\n- [[b:unary]] (1-1)\n- [[b:server-streaming]] (1 request → many responses)\n- [[b:client-streaming]] (many requests → 1 response)\n- [[b:bidirectional]] (both directions at once)\n\nGood for large data feeds, telemetry, chat, real-time sync.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Signature</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Unary</td><td>1 request → 1 response</td><td>ordinary RPC calls</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Server-streaming</td><td>1 → N</td><td>returning results incrementally: flight search, log tailing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Client-streaming</td><td>N → 1</td><td>batch upload, pushing metrics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bidirectional</td><td>N ↔ N</td><td>chat, two-way sync, real-time control</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::deep\n- [[g:server-streaming]]: returns multiple results incrementally (e.g. streaming flight search results — whichever airline responds first gets shown → faster UX than waiting to gather everything)\n- [[g:client-streaming]]: uploading/batching metrics\n- [[g:bidirectional]]: real-time sync/chat\n- streaming uses HTTP/2 flow control to bound in-flight bytes, but a framework write returning is not proof that the peer received the data\n- deadline expiry or cancellation can leave partial work, so propagate cancellation, check it in application code, and define retry/idempotency semantics (15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q2), (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4)\n\n- [[r:Gotcha]]: a long-lived stream holds connection resources → set a deadline/timeout; leaks if the client walks away and the server doesn't detect the cancellation\n- if it's really just 1 request-1 response, unary is simpler — don't reach for streaming just because it 'sounds cool'\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q4","q":"What API versioning strategy avoids breaking clients?","a":"- [[b:URI versioning]] (`/v1/...`) — explicit, widely used\n- [[b:Header/media-type versioning]] — cleaner URLs but harder to debug\n\n**Principles:**\n- [[g:backward-compatible]] changes (adding an optional field) → no new version needed\n- classify the change against real clients: additive changes may remain compatible, while changing meaning, removing fields, or changing error semantics can be [[r:breaking]]\n- a URI or header version is only a routing signal; contract checks, client observability, a parallel rollout, and a deprecation policy are what make retirement safe\n\n:::deep\n- favor [[g:backward-compatible evolution]]: add optional fields, don't change the meaning of existing fields, don't remove fields still in use (tolerant reader)\n- when a breaking change is unavoidable: run `/v1` and `/v2` in parallel, with a deprecation policy (`Sunset` header, partner notice, a deadline)\n- URI versioning is easiest to debug/route for a public API; header/media-type versioning keeps URLs clean but is harder to test. Neither strategy makes an incompatible schema safe by itself.\n\nOTA B2B deep dive: partners change slowly → support old versions for a long time; use *consumer-driven contract tests* (Pact) to know who still depends on which field before removing it.\n\n- The real process for retiring a version — announcement, `Sunset` header, measuring who's still calling it before turning it off (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4).\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q5","q":"How do you design pagination/filtering for a large list API?","a":"Use [[g:cursor/keyset pagination]] when the workload has a stable total order (a sequential column plus a unique id) and only next/previous navigation is needed. Large OFFSET can get slower and drift as rows change, but arbitrary page jumps or a search-engine token may justify another strategy.\n\nReturn a 'next' cursor. [[o:Whitelist]] filtering/sorting to avoid injection and un-indexed queries.\n\n:::deep\n- a large OFFSET scans-then-discards N rows → linearly slower, plus 'drift' when data changes between pages\n- [[g:keyset/cursor]] relies on a sequential column plus an id tie-breaker: `WHERE (created_at,id) &lt; (?,?) ORDER BY created_at DESC,id DESC LIMIT n` → stable & fast, uses the index well\n- return an encoded (opaque) cursor with an expiry/freshness policy; bind it to the filter, sort, and tenant scope, and reject invalid or expired cursors. Whitelist filter/sort fields.\n\n- OTA: read-heavy lists (bookings/transactions) should use keyset + a covering index; avoid an expensive total `COUNT(*)`\n- [[r:Keyset limitation]]: you can't jump straight to 'page N' (only next/prev). If arbitrary page jumps are required, use offset/page number or Elasticsearch's *search_after*; for mutating data, document freshness/snapshot semantics because stable ordering alone cannot promise a repeatable snapshot.\n- The DB side of this same question: why `COUNT(*)` is expensive and how keyset pagination performs on a table with tens of millions of rows (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q2).\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q6","q":"GraphQL vs REST — when does each fit?","a":"[[g:GraphQL]]: the client picks the fields, combining multiple resources in a single query → reduces over/under-fetching, good for complex UIs/many clients.\n\nTrade-offs:\n- [[r:caching is harder]]\n- risk of heavy queries (needs depth/complexity limits)\n- N+1 at the resolver (needs deliberate batching/identity-map controls; DataLoader is a common tool, not a specification requirement)\n\nREST is simpler, with better HTTP caching for most CRUD use cases.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>REST</th><th>GraphQL</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Who picks fields</td><td>server</td><td>client</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Round-trips for a complex screen</td><td>many</td><td>one</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HTTP/CDN cache</td><td>built-in: ETag, TTL</td><td>must build yourself: persisted queries</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Load limiting</td><td>per endpoint</td><td>per query depth and cost</td></tr>\n<tr><td>N+1</td><td>at the DB layer</td><td>an extra layer at the resolver; use deliberate batching/identity-map controls</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fits</td><td>public APIs, B2B, CRUD</td><td>multi-client apps, UIs aggregating many sources</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::deep\n- GraphQL: generic HTTP caching is harder because query shape matters (persisted queries and a query-aware cache can help), while heavy/deeply recursive queries need depth/complexity limits and cost-based rate limits.\n- resolver N+1 needs deliberate batching or an equivalent data-access plan; no particular library is mandatory.\n- REST: caches well (CDN/ETag), simple, clear status codes\n\nOTA deep dive: GraphQL fits mobile/web apps aggregating multiple sources (flights + pricing + seats + loyalty) in one round-trip; but B2B partner APIs + webhooks should still be REST. [[r:Don't use it for plain CRUD]] — the operational cost isn't worth it.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q7","q":"How do you benchmark REST and gRPC fairly, and what does flow control actually protect?","a":"Do not turn a serialization anecdote into a universal multiplier. Define the same workload on both paths: unary versus streaming, payload-size distribution, compression, TLS, connection reuse, concurrency, client/server language, CPU limits, and p50/p95/p99 targets.\n\nMeasure goodput, wire bytes, CPU/GC, queueing, cancellation, and error rate with the same topology.\n\nProtobuf and HTTP/2 may help an internal call, but a gateway translation, proxy, small messages, or a downstream bottleneck can dominate. A benchmark without these controls is a local observation, not an architecture rule.\n\nFor streaming, HTTP/2 flow control bounds bytes in flight per stream and connection; it is not a durable queue and not a delivery guarantee. A write accepted by the framework may still be buffered.\n\nUse bounded application buffers, explicit deadlines, cancellation propagation, and a read strategy that cannot deadlock a bidirectional stream.\n\n:::tip Ownership\nUse q3 for the four streaming shapes and 15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q2 for transport behaviour. The decision is evidence-led: choose the protocol that meets the measured contract with the smallest operational surface.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"}]},{"title":"Reactive / WebFlux","items":[{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q1","q":"Reactive programming (WebFlux): what are Mono/Flux, the event loop, and backpressure?","a":"WebFlux is built on Reactor:\n- [[b:Mono]] (0-1 element), [[b:Flux]] (0-N)\n- *non-blocking* processing on a small number of event-loop threads (Netty)\n- when there's no data, the thread isn't held → handles high I/O load with few threads\n\n[[g:Backpressure]]: a slow consumer tells the producer 'send only so much' (`request(n)`), avoiding memory overflow — a feature reactive streams have built-in that blocking models don't.\n\n:::warn Gotcha\njust one blocking call in a reactive chain [[r:blocks the event-loop thread handling that task]]. Enough such calls can stall available capacity and collapse tail latency; keep the chain non-blocking end to end (R2DBC driver, WebClient, etc.) or isolate blocking work explicitly.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- Reactor has both [[b:cold publishers]] and hot publishers. Many factory chains are lazy/cold until subscription, while shared sinks, processors, and some adapters can be hot. [[r:forgetting to subscribe = a cold chain never runs]]; verify the source and sharing policy before reasoning about execution.\n- `flatMap` runs concurrently and doesn't preserve order; `concatMap` preserves sequential order\n- backpressure strategies under overload: buffer/drop/latest/error\n- `publishOn` (switches the downstream thread) vs `subscribeOn` (switches the source thread)\n\n[[r:Fatal gotcha]]: a blocking call (JDBC, `RestTemplate`) blocks the Netty event-loop thread assigned to it; enough blocked workers can stall unrelated requests and tail latency.\n\nPrefer non-blocking dependencies (R2DBC, WebClient), or use `subscribeOn(boundedElastic)` with queue, deadline, and concurrency limits to isolate unavoidable blocking work.\n\nDeep dive: reactive stack traces are hard to read → turn on `Hooks.onOperatorDebug()`/checkpoints while debugging.\n\n**Executable proof that a blocking call left the event loop:**\n<pre><code>@Test\nvoid blockingWorkUsesBoundedElastic() {\n  Mono&lt;String&gt; result = Mono.fromCallable(() -&gt; {\n        Thread.sleep(20);                 // stand-in for blocking JDBC\n        return Thread.currentThread().getName();\n      })\n      .subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())\n      .timeout(Duration.ofMillis(200));\n  StepVerifier.create(result)\n      .assertNext(name -&gt; assertThat(name).contains(\"boundedElastic\"))\n      .verifyComplete();\n}\n# add reactor-test, then run the test with Maven or Gradle</code></pre>\n\nPut `subscribeOn` immediately after the blocking source; placing it later can leave subscription work on the event loop.\n\n- **Benefit:** Netty workers stay available.\n- **Limit:** `boundedElastic` moves and bounds blocking work; it does not make JDBC non-blocking. Its queue can still saturate, so keep a deadline, downstream concurrency limit and pending-work metrics.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q2","q":"Reactive (WebFlux) vs Virtual Threads — which should you pick in 2025–2026?","a":"Three ways to handle 'lots of I/O with few OS threads':\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>WebFlux (Reactor)</th><th>Vert.x</th><th>Virtual Threads</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Model</td><td>reactive streams</td><td>event loop + callback/Future</td><td>blocking style, JVM unmounts on supported I/O</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Code</td><td>Mono/Flux, harder to read</td><td>callback/Verticle, prone to callback hell</td><td>sequential, easiest to read</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Backpressure</td><td>built-in (request(n))</td><td>manual (pause/resume/fetch)</td><td>no reactive demand signal; use bounded queues/semaphores</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blocking code</td><td>unsafe on the event loop; isolate it</td><td>unsafe on the event loop → executeBlocking/worker</td><td>blocking I/O is supported; still bound CPU, pinning, and downstream</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ecosystem</td><td>Spring reactive</td><td>its own Vert.x ecosystem, polyglot</td><td>reuses JDBC/Hibernate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Debugging</td><td>operator context/checkpoints; async traces need discipline</td><td>event-loop context and async traces</td><td>sequential stack style, but inspect pinning and executor saturation</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nSpring Boot is not a fourth I/O model: it packages and configures Spring MVC or WebFlux.\n\nWith a Spring Boot release that supports the feature (for example, Boot 3.2+ on Java 21+), `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` can make the Servlet/Tomcat/Jetty path use virtual threads; it does not turn MVC into WebFlux.\n\n**For a 2025–2026 decision:** choose from the workload and dependency contract, not from a popularity ranking:\n- existing imperative Spring MVC + JDBC/JPA with mostly request-response and I/O waits → try MVC + virtual threads, then measure\n- end-to-end non-blocking clients/drivers, streaming, or a real reactive demand contract → WebFlux\n- explicit event-loop/multi-reactor control, polyglot services, or Vert.x integrations → Vert.x\n\n:::tip Bottom line\nPick the model by dependency and semantics:\n- virtual threads simplify blocking-style request-response code\n- WebFlux makes reactive streams and backpressure explicit\n- Vert.x gives direct event-loop/toolkit control\n\nNone of them removes the need to bound database connections, HTTP concurrency, broker consumers, or partner quotas.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- [[b:WebFlux & Vert.x]] are both [[o:event-loop]]-based: a blocking call stalls the event-loop thread handling that task; enough blocked workers can [[r:stall the service]]. Isolate it on a bounded scheduler, `executeBlocking`, a worker verticle, or another explicit executor.\n- Vert.x is lightweight, polyglot, has a distributed event bus — strong for gateways/high-throughput; but callback/Future style is prone to [[r:callback hell]] (mitigated with RxJava/Mutiny)\n- Reactor is built for *streaming composition* (time/window operators) with backpressure built in; Vert.x backpressure must be handled manually (`ReadStream.pause/resume/fetch`)\n- [[g:Virtual threads]] let teams write sequential blocking code and reuse JDBC/Hibernate. They have no reactive demand signal, so use connection pools, semaphores, bounded queues, and deadlines to protect scarce resources\n\n- for Kafka/RabbitMQ listeners, listener threading and broker semantics are independent: a Spring listener may use platform or virtual threads, while a Vert.x callback arrives on an event loop\n- Kafka bookkeeping is a committed offset; RabbitMQ bookkeeping is a consumer ack. Neither is business completion unless it follows the idempotent side effect\n\nKeep reactive/Vert.x for a true stream, backpressure, or event-loop contract; use MVC/virtual threads when imperative blocking dependencies make the simpler model the safer fit. The shared rule remains: [[r:never block the event loop]].\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q3","q":"Reactor: error handling and commonly used operators?","a":"**Operators:** `map/flatMap` (transform/chain), `zip` (combine), `filter`, `retryWhen` (retry with backoff), `timeout`.\n\n**Error handling:** `onErrorResume` (fallback), `onErrorMap` (convert exception), `doOnError` (side-effect logging).\n\n[[o:Errors propagate down the chain]] → handlers must be placed at the right point.\n\n:::deep\n- `onErrorResume` (fall back to another publisher — e.g. cached pricing when the GDS errors out)\n- `onErrorReturn` (a default value); `onErrorMap` (convert to a domain exception)\n- `retryWhen(Retry.backoff(n, duration).jitter(...))` (backoff+jitter, filtering which error types are retryable)\n- `timeout` + fallback to avoid hanging; `doOnError` is side-effect only (logging), not handling\n- combine with resilience4j (circuit breaker/bulkhead) at the reactive layer\n\nDeep dive: fanning out to multiple GDSs in parallel → `flatMap` + a per-call timeout + `onErrorResume(empty)` so [[g:'one airline failing doesn't take down the whole search']].\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q4","q":"WebSocket vs SSE — how do you choose for real-time features?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>SSE</th><th>WebSocket</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Direction</td><td>one-way server→client</td><td>full-duplex, both ways</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transport</td><td>HTTP (text/event-stream)</td><td>ws:// (upgraded from HTTP)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reconnect</td><td>automatic (Last-Event-ID)</td><td>must be coded yourself</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good for</td><td>feeds, notifications, price pushes</td><td>chat, collaborative apps, games</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nChoose based on whether you need two-way communication: [[g:SSE is cheaper and simpler]] if you only need server push.\n\n:::deep\n- SSE is one-way, with auto-reconnect via `Last-Event-ID` to resume — enough for 'push real-time price/seat/booking status updates'\n- [[r:SSE gotcha]]: browser HTTP/1.1 per-origin connection limits (often six) are user-agent-specific; HTTP/2 multiplexing changes the constraint, and proxy buffering can still block the stream.\n- test the actual browser, HTTP version, and proxy before choosing a connection budget (disable buffering in Nginx when appropriate).\n- [[r:WebSocket gotcha]]: doesn't auto-reconnect (the client must code reconnection & state recovery itself), needs heartbeat ping/pong to detect dead connections, an LB/proxy idle-timeout silently kills the connection &#8594; keep periodic pings\n- scaling: sticky sessions are needed only when connection state is local. A shared pub/sub layer or externalized session/resume state can remove that dependency; test LB routing and reconnect behaviour (see 16.9).\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q5","q":"What benefits does a contract-first approach with OpenAPI/Protobuf bring?","a":"Define the [[g:API contract]] first (OpenAPI for REST, .proto for gRPC):\n- generates client/server stubs, mocks, contract tests\n- lets FE/BE teams work in parallel\n- reduces misunderstandings\n\nThe contract is the shared language between BA/FE/designers.\n\n:::deep\n- contract-first → a 'shared language' for BE/FE/partners: generates stubs, mock servers; *contract tests* catch breaking changes in CI before deploy\n- FE/BE work in parallel off the spec; versioning & docs are auto-generated (Swagger UI)\n- OTA/B2B: the spec is a commitment; use *consumer-driven contracts* (Pact) to know which fields partners depend on before changing them\n\n[[r:Gotcha]]: the spec drifts from the code if it's generated once and then hand-edited.\n\n**A contract-first gate should:**\n- lint the schema and run breaking-change checks\n- compile generated clients/servers\n- execute provider/consumer contract tests\n- run a runtime integration test\n\nEither generate from the spec or fail when generated output is stale.\n\n- The contract is exactly a [[g:Published Language]] in DDD terms — the shared language at the boundary between two bounded contexts, so it must be treated as a real contract, not something edited on a whim (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q3).\n- The contract's lifecycle after it's signed off: adding an optional field breaks nobody; changing the meaning of a field or removing it requires a retirement process with a `Sunset` header and measuring who's still calling it (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4).\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q6","q":"How do you keep a reactive service safe when a downstream or stream is slower than the producer?","a":"Backpressure is a signal to reduce demand, not permission to create an unlimited queue. Put a bound on every buffer and concurrency gate, then choose the overflow contract deliberately:\n- wait within a deadline\n- drop only data that is explicitly disposable\n- return a degraded result\n- reject with a retryable error\n\nPropagate cancellation when the client disconnects or the deadline expires.\n\nA reactive operator can preserve a signal while an adapter, broker client, database pool, or bounded-elastic scheduler still saturates; instrument queue depth, pending demand, in-flight work, downstream latency, cancellation, and dropped or rejected items.\n\nNever block the event loop to make a slow dependency look synchronous. Isolate unavoidable blocking work on a bounded scheduler, cap its queue, and protect the dependency with a bulkhead.\n\nIf the consumer needs a replayable history rather than live flow control, use a durable cursor or job contract instead of relying on a live stream.\n\n:::deep\n<code>request(n)</code> controls demand between reactive publishers; it does not make a database, network, or business side effect cancellable automatically. Cancellation and partial-work semantics belong in the adapter contract.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"}]}]},"hasVi":true,"stem":"04-rest-grpc-webflux","sourceOwners":{"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12":"design-review-framewo